<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Risk Measures</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix, we provide formal definitions of the various classes of risk measures used in the context of RL, where each (non-mutually-exclusive) class of risk measures can be thought of as satisfying a specific set of mathematical properties:
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.1</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Coherent Risk Measure; adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Artzner et al. [1999]</a></cite>)

A risk measure, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, is called coherent if it satisfies the following four axioms for all <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,X^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,X^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="A1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Monotonicity: If </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leq X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leq X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> almost surely, then </span><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(X)\leq\rho(X^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(X)\leq\rho(X^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Translation Invariance: For all </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(X+c)=\rho(X)+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(X+c)=\rho(X)+c</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Positive Homogeneity: For all </span><math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\lambda X)=\lambda\rho(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\lambda X)=\lambda\rho(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Subadditivity: </span><math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(X+X^{\prime})\leq\rho(X)+\rho(X^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(X+X^{\prime})\leq\rho(X)+\rho(X^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Coherent risk measures are useful because they enforce a form of self-consistency in how risk is quantified and compared. In particular, monotonicity ensures that if a random variable, <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, always yields outcomes that are no worse than outcomes induced by another random variable, <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> should be considered less risky than <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Translation invariance requires that adding a constant amount to <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> simply shifts its risk by that same amount. Positive homogeneity enforces scale-consistency, such that doubling the size of <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> also doubles its risk. Finally, subadditivity formalizes the idea of diversification, requiring that the risk of two combined random variables cannot exceed the sum of their individual risks. We note that the positive homogeneity and subadditivity properties also ensure that coherent risk measures are convex.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.2</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Static Risk Measure; adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Artzner et al. [1999]</a></cite>)

Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math> denote a probability space, and let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{N}\subseteq\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{N}\subseteq\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra representing information available at time <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> the space of essentially bounded, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>-measurable random variables. A static risk measure is a mapping, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, that assigns to each random variable, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>, a single real value.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In essence, a static risk measure evaluates risk at a fixed point in time, without taking into consideration the temporal evolution of information. In RL-based contexts, static risk measures can be useful for quantifying the risk associated with the return at the end of an episode. One of the primary appeals of static risk measures is that they are considered to be easily interpretable.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.3</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Conditional Risk Measure; adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruszczyński [2010]</a></cite>)

Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math> denote a probability space with filtration <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{t})_{t=0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{t})_{t=0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}\subseteq\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}\subseteq\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the information available up to time <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> the space of essentially bounded, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>-measurable random variables, and by <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> the space of essentially bounded, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>-measurable random variables. A conditional risk measure at time <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})\to L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})\to L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, that assigns to each random variable, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})</annotation></semantics></math>, a conditional risk evaluation, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>-measurable and satisfies the following monotonicity property: if <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leq X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leq X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely, then <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}(X)\leq\rho_{t}(X^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}(X)\leq\rho_{t}(X^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In essence, a conditional risk measure at time <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping that evaluates the risk of future outcomes (e.g. at time <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N&gt;t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N&gt;t</annotation></semantics></math>) based on the information available up to and including time <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The monotonicity property ensures that if a future outcome, <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, always yields less loss (or more reward) than another outcome, <math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is never assigned a higher risk than <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.4</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Dynamic Risk Measure; adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruszczyński [2010]</a></cite>)

Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math> denote a probability space with filtration <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{t})_{t=0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{t})_{t=0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. A dynamic risk measure is a <em id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">sequence</em> of conditional risk measures, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\rho_{t}\}_{t=0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\rho_{t}\}_{t=0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})\to L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})\to L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> assigns to every random variable <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> a conditional risk evaluation, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="A1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>-measurable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In essence, a dynamic risk measure provides a time-indexed family of risk assessments, allowing risk to be tracked and updated as new information becomes available. In RL-based contexts, dynamic risk measures can be useful for capturing the sequential nature of decision-making (i.e., that actions taken at each time step can potentially influence future outcomes, and hence, future risk evaluations).
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.5</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Time-Consistent Risk Measure; adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boda and Filar [2006]</a></cite>)

Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\rho_{t}\}_{t=0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\rho_{t}\}_{t=0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be a dynamic risk measure defined on a probability space <math id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math> with filtration <math id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{F}_{t})_{t=0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{F}_{t})_{t=0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The dynamic risk measure is said to be time-consistent if, for all random variables <math id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,X^{\prime}\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,X^{\prime}\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="A1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ \rho_{t+1}(X)\leq\rho_{t+1}(X^{\prime})\implies\rho_{t}(X)\leq\rho_{t}(X^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ \rho_{t+1}(X)\leq\rho_{t+1}(X^{\prime})\implies\rho_{t}(X)\leq\rho_{t}(X^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Time-consistent risk measures can be appealing because they ensure that if one future outcome is deemed less risky than another at some time step, <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, then that same outcome is not deemed more risky than the other at any other time step. In RL-based contexts, time-consistent risk measures can be useful because they can induce Bellman-like recursions with appealing dynamic programming-like properties. One way to think about time consistency in RL-based contexts is to ask the question: <em id="A1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">can the agent change its mind about how risky something is based on new information?</em> If the answer is <em id="A1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">yes</em>, then there exists a lack of time consistency.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.6</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Nested Risk Measure; adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruszczyński [2010]</a></cite>)

A nested risk measure is a dynamic risk measure that is constructed recursively from one-step conditional risk measures. More formally, given conditional risk measures, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t+1})\to L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t+1})\to L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, a nested risk measure over time horizon <math id="A1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\rho_{0:N}(X)\doteq\rho_{0}\big(\rho_{1}(\cdots\rho_{N-1}(X)\cdots)\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≐</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{0:N}(X)\doteq\rho_{0}\big(\rho_{1}(\cdots\rho_{N-1}(X)\cdots)\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Nested risk measures are useful because they ensure time consistency. That is, the risk evaluation at earlier times is consistent with future evaluations. One of the drawbacks of nested risk measures is that they are typically hard to interpret. We note that typically in the RL literature, the terms ‘dynamic risk measure’ and ‘nested risk measure’ are used interchangeably; however, formally speaking, dynamic risk measures need not be time-consistent, nor have the nested structure.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition A.7</span></span><span id="A1.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Markov Risk Measure; adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruszczyński [2010]</a></cite>)

Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math> denote a probability space, and let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{S_{t}\}_{t=0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S_{t}\}_{t=0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a Markov process where each state, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}:\Omega\to\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}:\Omega\to\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, takes values in a measurable state-space <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{S}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{S}))</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}\doteq\sigma(S_{0},\ldots,S_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}\doteq\sigma(S_{0},\ldots,S_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Borel <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra on <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. A one-step conditional risk measure, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t+1})\to L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}:L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t+1})\to L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, is called a Markov risk measure if, for every <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in L^{\infty}(\mathcal{F}_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, the risk assessment satisfies <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}(X)\in L^{\infty}(\sigma(S_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}(X)\in L^{\infty}(\sigma(S_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(S_{t})\subseteq\sigma(S_{0},\ldots,S_{t})=\mathcal{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(S_{t})\subseteq\sigma(S_{0},\ldots,S_{t})=\mathcal{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, the risk assessment <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(S_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(S_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>-measurable, such that it only depends on the current state, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Markov risk measures are useful because they enforce a one-step time dependence structure that makes them compatible with MDP-based RL solution methods. From a risk perspective, this means that the assessment of risk at each time step only depends on the information available at that time step, rather than the entire history of past information. Note that in an MDP setting (as opposed to the simpler Markov process described in Definition <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem7" title="Definition A.7. ‣ Appendix A Risk Measures ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>), the ‘state’ can be characterized as a state-action pair. That is, <math id="A1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}(X)\in L^{\infty}(\sigma(S_{t},A_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}(X)\in L^{\infty}(\sigma(S_{t},A_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in a measurable action-space, <math id="A1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Numerical Experiments</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix contains details regarding the numerical experiments performed as part of this work. The overall aim of the experiments was to provide a concrete example of an ergodic risk measure being optimized in a continual learning setting. In particular, we focused on the well-known conditional value-at-risk (CVaR) risk measure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rockafellar and Uryasev, 2000</a>]</cite>. More formally, consider a random variable <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with a finite mean on a probability space <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})</annotation></semantics></math>, and with a cumulative distribution function <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x)=\mathbb{P}(X\leq x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x)=\mathbb{P}(X\leq x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The (left-tail) <em id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">value-at-risk (VaR)</em> of <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with parameter <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-quantile of <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{VaR}_{\tau}(X)=\sup\{x\mid F(x)\leq\tau\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>VaR</mtext><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{VaR}_{\tau}(X)=\sup\{x\mid F(x)\leq\tau\}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous at <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\text{VaR}_{\tau}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>VaR</mtext><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\text{VaR}_{\tau}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CVaR}_{\tau}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CVaR}_{\tau}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as the expected value of <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on <math id="A2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> being less than or equal to <math id="A2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{VaR}_{\tau}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>VaR</mtext><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{VaR}_{\tau}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="A2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CVaR}_{\tau}(X)=\mathbb{E}[X\mid X\leq\text{VaR}_{\tau}(X)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>VaR</mtext><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CVaR}_{\tau}(X)=\mathbb{E}[X\mid X\leq\text{VaR}_{\tau}(X)]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As per Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Case Study: CVaR as an Ergodic Risk Measure ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the CVaR risk measure can be formulated as the continual learning objective (<a href="#S5.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 5 Case Study: CVaR as an Ergodic Risk Measure ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), which is displayed below as Equation (<a href="#A2.E1" title="Equation B.1 ‣ Appendix B Numerical Experiments ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>) for convenience:</p>
<table id="A2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{CVaR}_{\pi_{t}}\doteq\lim_{n\rightarrow{}\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=t_{0}}^{n}\text{CVaR}[R_{t}\mid S_{t}\sim\mu_{\pi_{t}},A_{t}\sim\pi_{t}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≐</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CVaR}_{\pi_{t}}\doteq\lim_{n\rightarrow{}\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=t_{0}}^{n}\text{CVaR}[R_{t}\mid S_{t}\sim\mu_{\pi_{t}},A_{t}\sim\pi_{t}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">That is, we aimed to optimize the (left-tail) conditional value-at-risk associated with the limiting per-step reward distribution induced when following stationary policy <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. More specifically, our aim was to optimize the CVaR objective (<a href="#A2.E1" title="Equation B.1 ‣ Appendix B Numerical Experiments ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>) in two continual learning tasks via the <em id="A2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">RED CVaR Q-learning</em> algorithm proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rojas and Lee [2025]</a></cite>. In particular, the RED CVaR Q-learning algorithm was designed to optimize the CVaR associated with the long-run per-step reward distribution of an average-reward MDP, which precisely corresponds to the continual learning objective (<a href="#A2.E1" title="Equation B.1 ‣ Appendix B Numerical Experiments ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>). The RED CVaR Q-learning algorithm (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Numerical Experiments ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is shown below:</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> RED CVaR Q-Learning (Tabular) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rojas and Lee, 2025</a>]</cite></figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> the policy <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> to be used (e.g., <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm parameters:</span> step size parameters <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>CVaR</mtext></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; CVaR parameter <math id="alg1.l2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           Initialize <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s,a)\&gt;\forall s,a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.387em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s,a)\&gt;\forall s,a</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. to zero)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           Initialize CVaR arbitrarily (e.g. to zero)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           Initialize VaR arbitrarily (e.g. to zero)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           Obtain initial <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> still time to train <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> action given by <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="alg1.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Take action <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, observe <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R,S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R,S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}=\text{VaR}-\frac{1}{\tau}\max\{\text{VaR}-R,0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}=\text{VaR}-\frac{1}{\tau}\max\{\text{VaR}-R,0\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\tilde{R}-\text{CVaR}+\max_{a}Q(S^{\prime},a)-Q(S,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mtext>CVaR</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\tilde{R}-\text{CVaR}+\max_{a}Q(S^{\prime},a)-Q(S,A)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(S,A)=Q(S,A)+\alpha\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(S,A)=Q(S,A)+\alpha\delta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{CVaR}=\text{CVaR}+\alpha_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>CVaR</mtext></msub></msub><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CVaR}=\text{CVaR}+\alpha_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\delta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\geq\text{VaR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mtext>VaR</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\geq\text{VaR}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
             <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{VaR}=\text{VaR}+\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}(\delta+\text{CVaR}-\text{VaR})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>VaR</mtext><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mo>−</mo><mtext>VaR</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{VaR}=\text{VaR}+\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}(\delta+\text{CVaR}-\text{VaR})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>


</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
             <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{VaR}=\text{VaR}+\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\left(\left(\frac{\tau}{\tau-1}\right)\delta+\text{CVaR}-\text{VaR}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>VaR</mtext><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext></msub></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mo>−</mo><mtext>VaR</mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{VaR}=\text{VaR}+\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\left(\left(\frac{\tau}{\tau-1}\right)\delta+\text{CVaR}-\text{VaR}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           return <math id="alg1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of the two continual learning tasks considered in this work, we considered two continual variations of the <em id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">red-pill blue-pill (RPBP)</em> task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rojas and Lee, 2025</a>]</cite>. More specifically, in the regular (non-continual) RPBP task, an agent, at each time step, can take either a ‘red pill’, which takes them to the ‘red world’ state, or a ‘blue pill’, which takes them to the ‘blue world’ state. Each state has its own characteristic per-step reward distribution, such that for a sufficiently low CVaR parameter, <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the red world state has a reward distribution with a lower (worse) mean but a higher (better) CVaR compared to the blue world state. That is, in the regular RPBP task, for a sufficiently low CVaR parameter, <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, we would expect a risk-neutral agent to learn a policy that prefers to stay in the blue world, and a risk-averse agent to learn a policy that prefers to stay in the red world.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now discuss the two continual variations of the RPBP task considered in this work:</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span><math id="A2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-RPBP Task</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the first task, we considered a continual variation of the RPBP task, such that the <em id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">risk attitude</em> of the agent, which is governed by the CVaR parameter, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, changes over time from risk-neutral (<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>) to risk-averse (<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>). In particular, we would expect that the agent first learns to stay in the blue world state, but then changes its preference to the red world state as its risk attitude changes from risk-neutral to risk-averse. More formally, this task can be viewed as a continual learning task, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{M}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{M}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a changing reward function, such that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{k}\doteq\langle\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R}_{k},p\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{k}\doteq\langle\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R}_{k},p\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="A2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}_{t,k}=\text{VaR}_{t}-\frac{1}{\tau_{{}_{k}}}(\text{VaR}_{t}-R_{t})^{+}\text{ (see Algorithm \ref{alg_cvar_1})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>VaR</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>VaR</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> (see Algorithm </mtext><mtext><a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Numerical Experiments ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}_{t,k}=\text{VaR}_{t}-\frac{1}{\tau_{{}_{k}}}(\text{VaR}_{t}-R_{t})^{+}\text{ (see Algorithm \ref{alg_cvar_1})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(B.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau_{{}_{1}}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{{}_{1}}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau_{{}_{2}}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{{}_{2}}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. The indexing function, <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, was defined such that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(t)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(t)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;50,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>50,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;50,000</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(t)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(t)=2</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. That is, the agent’s risk attitude changes from risk-neutral to risk-averse at <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=50,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=50,000</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of the hyperparameters used with the RED CVaR Q-learning algorithm, we used the tuned hyperparameters from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rojas and Lee [2025]</a></cite>. That is, <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\text{2e-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>2e-2</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\text{2e-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\doteq\eta_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>CVaR</mtext></msub></msub><mo>≐</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>CVaR</mtext></msub></msub><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\doteq\eta_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}=\text{1e-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>CVaR</mtext></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>1e-1</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}=\text{1e-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\doteq\eta_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext></msub></msub><mo>≐</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext></msub></msub><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\doteq\eta_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}=\text{1e-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>1e-1</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}=\text{1e-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We used an <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy policy with a fixed epsilon of 0.1, and set all initial guesses to zero. The results for this <em id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="A2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-RPBP</em> task are shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5 Case Study: CVaR as an Ergodic Risk Measure ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span><math id="A2.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>-RPBP Task</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the second task, we considered another variation of the RPBP task. In this variation, the characteristic per-step reward distributions of the states change over time, such that the agent is required to continually adapt and find the state with the better CVaR (given a fixed risk attitude, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>). More formally, this task can be viewed as a continual learning task with a changing state-space, such that a given state is effectively replaced with a state with a different per-step reward distribution. That is, we have a continual learning task, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{M}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{M}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{k}\doteq\langle\mathcal{S}_{k},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R},p\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{k}\doteq\langle\mathcal{S}_{k},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R},p\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, for a given <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the red world state reward distribution is characterized as a Gaussian distribution with mean, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{red}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>red</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{red}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and standard deviation, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{red}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>red</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{red}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, the blue world state reward distribution is characterized as a mixture of two Gaussian distributions with means, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{blue-a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>blue-a</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{blue-a}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{blue-b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>blue-b</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{blue-b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, standard deviations, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{blue-a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>blue-a</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{blue-a}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{blue-b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>blue-b</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{blue-b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a mixing coefficient of 0.5.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the experiment performed, we set <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and all states, we set the standard deviation to 0.05. For <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{red}}=\text{-0.7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>red</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>-0.7</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{red}}=\text{-0.7}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{blue-a}}=\text{-1.0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>blue-a</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>-1.0</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{blue-a}}=\text{-1.0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{blue-b}}=\text{-0.2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>blue-b</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>-0.2</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{blue-b}}=\text{-0.2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{red}}=\text{-1.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>red</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>-1.5</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{red}}=\text{-1.5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{blue-a}}=\text{-1.25}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>blue-a</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>-1.25</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{blue-a}}=\text{-1.25}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{blue-b}}=\text{-1.0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>blue-b</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>-1.0</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{blue-b}}=\text{-1.0}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{red}}=\text{-0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>red</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>-0.5</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{red}}=\text{-0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{blue-a}}=\text{-0.9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>blue-a</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>-0.9</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{blue-a}}=\text{-0.9}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{blue-b}}=\text{-0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>blue-b</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>-0.5</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{blue-b}}=\text{-0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>. The indexing function, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, was defined such that <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(t)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(t)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;40,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>40,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;40,000</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(t)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(t)=2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40,000\leq t&lt;80,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40,000</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>80,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40,000\leq t&lt;80,000</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(t)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(t)=3</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of the hyperparameters used with the RED CVaR Q-learning algorithm, we used the tuned hyperparameters from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rojas and Lee [2025]</a></cite>. That is, <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\text{2e-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>2e-2</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\text{2e-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\doteq\eta_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>CVaR</mtext></msub></msub><mo>≐</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>CVaR</mtext></msub></msub><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\doteq\eta_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}=\text{1e-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>CVaR</mtext></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>1e-1</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{{}_{\text{CVaR}}}=\text{1e-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\doteq\eta_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext></msub></msub><mo>≐</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext></msub></msub><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\doteq\eta_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}=\text{1e-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>VaR</mtext></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>1e-1</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{{}_{\text{VaR}}}=\text{1e-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We used a fixed CVaR parameter, <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, of 0.25, an <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy policy with a fixed epsilon of 0.1, and set all initial guesses to zero. The results for this <em id="A2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="A2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>-RPBP</em> task are shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5 Case Study: CVaR as an Ergodic Risk Measure ‣ Ergodic Risk Measures:Towards a Risk-Aware Foundation forContinual Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
